<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molly Brant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1736–<a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Konwatsi'tsiaiénni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Molly Brant was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> leader of the 18th century, the consort of the British Indian superintendent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a> and the elder sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>. A respected clan matron, she used her influence to keep the Mohawk and much of the Iroquois Confederacy loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. After the war she settled at Kingston in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, and she is honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> as a person of national historic significance.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is certain about Brant's early life. Named Mary, she was born to Christian Mohawk parents, possibly at Canajoharie or in the Ohio Country, where her family lived for a time. Her name Konwatsiʼtsiaiénni means Someone Lends Her a Flower; Degonwadonti, Two Against One, was given at adulthood. In 1753 her mother married Brant Kanagaradunkwa, a Mohawk royaner of the Turtle clan, whose name Molly and Joseph took. The family lived in a colonial-style frame house and attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and Molly spoke Mohawk and English, though she may have been only semi-literate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She became the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a>, the Superintendent for Northern Indian Affairs, and bore their son Peter Warren Johnson in September 1759; eight of their nine children lived past infancy. At Fort Johnson and later Johnson Hall she ran the household and estate during his long absences, and Johnson used the tie in his dealings with the Haudenosaunee. After his death in July 1774 she returned to Canajoharie and prospered as a fur trader.</p><p>A Loyalist, she sheltered Loyalists fleeing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. In August 1777 she sent runners to warn the British that Patriot militia were marching to relieve Fort Stanwix, which led to the ambush at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oriskany">Battle of Oriskany</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> and Patriots then plundered Canajoharie and she fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, where she urged the council to stay loyal to the Crown against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> chief Sayenqueraghta. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and Carleton Island she worked as an intermediary between the British and the Haudenosaunee; the British commander there thought her influence far greater than that of all their chiefs together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1783 she moved to Cataraqui, now Kingston, where the British gave her a house, a pension of £100 a year and land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> ruled that she and her children, as Indians, could not own the land Johnson had left them. She died at Kingston on 16 April 1796 and was buried in St. Paul's Churchyard, the grave now lost. Long ignored by historians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, she has drawn scholarly interest since the late 20th century; some criticise her as pro-British at the Haudenosaunee's expense, while others hold that she chose what she believed best for her people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molly Brant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>No birthright made a war chief: valour in war was a prerequisite for any man to be made a chief, and patronage counted, as <b>Molly Brant</b>’s did when her brother Joseph became a war chief.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Hendrick Theyanoguin, <b>Molly Brant</b> and Joseph Brant built close ties with Britain and the Indian superintendent William Johnson.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingston-ontario">Kingston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.23,-76.48&amp;year=1775" aria-label="Kingston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2719 598 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Molly Brant"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3169 868h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3169 868h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.23,-76.48&amp;year=1775">What was here in 1775? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
